1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,050
Welcome to Brevio Studio.

2
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:08,550
This walkthrough follows one lecture, from choosing the source to a checked, approved video.

3
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:14,100
Start by choosing who the video is for: researchers, the general public or kids.

4
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:18,525
Here we pick kids, so the voice, tone and cards will suit children.

5
00:00:18,825 --> 00:00:23,475
Under Advanced settings you will find the options most people leave alone.

6
00:00:23,675 --> 00:00:28,325
Aspect ratio sets the video shape: landscape, portrait or square.

7
00:00:28,525 --> 00:00:31,950
Transcription quality trades speed for accuracy.

8
00:00:32,150 --> 00:00:36,725
The local model field names the model that picks clips on this computer.

9
00:00:36,925 --> 00:00:41,300
Minimum and maximum clip length limit how long each clip can be.

10
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:43,900
Finally, paste the source link here.

11
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:47,325
It is credited on the ending card and in the post.

12
00:00:47,625 --> 00:00:49,650
Now click Analyze source.

13
00:00:49,850 --> 00:00:54,650
Brevio transcribes the lecture on this computer and lists its strongest clips.

14
00:00:54,950 --> 00:00:57,950
Each suggested clip has a Keep checkbox.

15
00:00:58,150 --> 00:01:02,200
Untick any clip you do not want, or tick it again to bring it back.

16
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:04,925
Select a clip to see it in the player.

17
00:01:05,125 --> 00:01:08,000
Play it, and click Set in where the clip should start.

18
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:10,250
Click Set out where it should end.

19
00:01:10,550 --> 00:01:12,675
Now click Generate preview.

20
00:01:12,875 --> 00:01:16,650
Brevio first plans a blueprint, before it writes anything.

21
00:01:16,850 --> 00:01:24,975
The bar shows the planned opening, clips, bridges and ending, with an estimated length inside the 30 to 60 second budget.

22
00:01:25,175 --> 00:01:29,400
Below it are the ideas for the opening, each bridge and the ending.

23
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:32,200
Then it builds the video from that plan.

24
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:35,575
In the Studio, press play to watch the preview.

25
00:01:35,775 --> 00:01:39,925
The timeline lists the opening, each clip, the bridges and the ending.

26
00:01:40,225 --> 00:01:44,850
Open Source evidence to see the excerpt behind the selected section.

27
00:01:45,050 --> 00:01:50,775
Open Blueprint to see the plan again, with a result line showing the real clip count and length.

28
00:01:51,075 --> 00:01:53,350
A named person types their name.

29
00:01:53,550 --> 00:01:56,200
They confirm they reviewed the preview.

30
00:01:56,400 --> 00:02:00,200
Then they approve it, and nothing can be exported before that.

31
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:02,550
Now click Export video.

32
00:02:02,750 --> 00:02:06,900
On the Export page, download the MP4 or the ZIP package.

33
00:02:07,100 --> 00:02:10,100
Open Post copy to copy the post text.

34
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:15,050
The technical check covers media, captions, loudness and narration.

35
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:19,350
Open any item to see what it checks and what your export shows.

36
00:02:19,650 --> 00:02:23,650
The structure check covers section order, bridges and narration.

37
00:02:23,850 --> 00:02:30,250
Five blueprint checks compare the export with the plan: clip count, clips, bridges, length and wording.

38
00:02:30,450 --> 00:02:35,525
Wording is compared by key words, because the video text is shortened on purpose.

39
00:02:35,725 --> 00:02:41,775
Each section is marked as following the plan or differing, and only a clear drift asks for review.

40
00:02:42,075 --> 00:02:46,725
That is the whole workflow: choose, trim, preview, approve and export.

41
00:02:46,925 --> 00:02:50,350
A person reviews every video before it goes out.
